# hi's own flags, one row each:
#   <flag>|<argument>|<needs>|<script var>|<first arg>|<help>
#
# The one place they are spelled. hi.sh reads it to dispatch (a row with a
# script var execs ${!var} with the arg first; the rest are hi.sh's own case
# arms) and to print --help; common/targets.sh reads it so `hi --<TAB>` offers
# the same list. <argument> is what the flag takes on the command line, as
# --help shows it (empty for a bare flag); the shape is part of the 1.x
# contract, so it lives here rather than in prose. <needs> is what a flag
# wants that the payload does not carry: `-` for nothing (works in a
# session), `scripts` for scripts/ (a package ships it, a session does not),
# `git` for .git (neither a package nor a session has it). docs/hi.1 is the
# long form and is drift-checked against this by tests/hi/parse_test.sh.
--help||-|||this text
--version||-|||the version: stamped at packaging time, or git describe
--preview-packages||-|||the package-priority legend, as the header prints it
--doctor|[--json] [target]|scripts|_HI_DOCTOR||a read-only pre-flight report: the tree, the overlay, the shell rc files parsed, every backend probed, and with a target, what it resolves to; --json for a bug report
--install||scripts|_HI_INSTALL||install or repair say-hi's lines in your shell rc files
--uninstall||scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--uninstall|take those lines back out again
--configure||scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--features-only|revisit the feature toggles, leaving the rc wiring be
--overlay-init||scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--overlay-init|seed the config overlay with the shipped defaults and git it, in place
--preview-colors||scripts|_HI_COLOR_PREVIEW||what every ssh host and your user resolve to, in color
--update||git|||git pull in the say-hi checkout (needs its .git)
--use|<arm>|-|||force that arm for <target> and skip every probe: ssh, or a backend name - docker, podman, nerdctl, finch, nomad, kube
--plain||-|||skip the payload and hand over a bare shell - no tar, no base64, no writable /tmp, no $HOME
